1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,334
This demonstration shows MySQL Cluster Set across independent community OpenShift clusters in London and

2
00:00:07,344 --> 00:00:13,835
Frankfurt. We first tour the real consoles, with private fields masked. We then use

3
00:00:13,845 --> 00:00:19,382
an illustrative terminal replay to explain creation, replication, and a controlled switch of the

4
00:00:19,392 --> 00:00:20,353
active writer.

5
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:29,493
This is the London OKD console. The MySQL Operator deployment lives in the mysql-operator

6
00:00:29,503 --> 00:00:36,051
namespace. This local controller manages London database resources. It does not manage the Frankfurt

7
00:00:36,061 --> 00:00:37,367
Kubernetes API.

8
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:46,277
Frankfurt has its own MySQL Operator in a namespace with the same name. These

9
00:00:46,287 --> 00:00:53,605
are independent controllers. The bridge between sites is MySQL Cluster Set replication over reachable

10
00:00:53,615 --> 00:00:58,048
database endpoints, rather than one shared Kubernetes controller.

11
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:08,216
The Submariner namespace contains its controller and Lighthouse deployments. Submariner gateways and route integration

12
00:01:08,226 --> 00:01:15,690
carry cross-cluster traffic. The London broker exchanges endpoint and service metadata. It is not

13
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:18,137
a relay for the MySQL data stream.

14
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:26,290
The Frankfurt console lists the active gateway resource. Its peer connection is checked separately

15
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:32,059
through the Gateway status and command line. The gateways use private routing between the

16
00:01:32,069 --> 00:01:37,428
regions. A connected tunnel alone does not prove that Lighthouse service records are synchronized.

17
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:46,713
Lighthouse watches Service Exports, then exchanges Service Import and Endpoint Slice metadata through the

18
00:01:46,723 --> 00:01:53,090
broker. This London database service is imported into Frankfurt. The source namespace must exist

19
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:57,713
at the destination. Stable pod names include the Submariner cluster identifier.

20
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:05,893
The OKD DNS Operator forwards the cluster set dot local suffix to the local

21
00:02:05,903 --> 00:02:12,722
Lighthouse DNS service. Lighthouse builds those service answers from synchronized metadata. It does not

22
00:02:12,732 --> 00:02:19,217
manage the OCI private DNS zones for the API, internal API, or application ingress

23
00:02:19,227 --> 00:02:19,703
routes.

24
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:28,430
We now move to the terminal replay. Prerequisites and protected root Secrets already exist.

25
00:02:28,987 --> 00:02:34,303
Each region has the local database namespace, and an empty peer namespace for Lighthouse

26
00:02:34,313 --> 00:02:39,983
imports. Names and output in this walkthrough are examples, not a recording of a

27
00:02:39,993 --> 00:02:41,187
new live installation.

28
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:49,681
Create the London InnoDB Cluster using its reviewed manifest. The local operator prepares the

29
00:02:49,691 --> 00:02:55,920
database and Router, then the cluster becomes online. In this Operator workflow, the initial

30
00:02:55,930 --> 00:03:01,392
Cluster Set domain is created as part of bootstrap. We read that existing domain

31
00:03:01,402 --> 00:03:02,820
from the center terminal.

32
00:03:04,960 --> 00:03:12,662
The headless database service is explicitly exported. Lighthouse synchronizes it into the matching peer

33
00:03:12,672 --> 00:03:20,179
namespace. From a Frankfurt pod, the qualified London member name resolves. The database manifest

34
00:03:20,189 --> 00:03:26,172
advertises that stable name, so remote MySQL members can reach it as well.

35
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:34,819
The Frankfurt manifest uses init DB cluster Set with the primary database target and

36
00:03:34,829 --> 00:03:41,557
a local root Secret reference. The Frankfurt sidecar invokes MySQL Admin API to create

37
00:03:41,567 --> 00:03:47,670
the replica cluster and configure its asynchronous channel. The two Operators continue managing their

38
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:49,583
own local Kubernetes resources.

39
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:58,107
Check each direct database member. London is writable, and Frankfurt has super read only

40
00:03:58,117 --> 00:04:04,518
enabled. Each single-member group has a local primary member, but their global Cluster Set

41
00:04:04,528 --> 00:04:10,160
roles differ. A Router in Frankfurt may still route to the global primary, so

42
00:04:10,170 --> 00:04:12,766
direct sessions are used for this verification.

43
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:19,378
In the replay, a demo row is written on the London primary and then

44
00:04:19,388 --> 00:04:26,841
read directly on the Frankfurt replica. This checks database replication over the connected network.

45
00:04:27,403 --> 00:04:31,641
DNS success by itself would not prove that the transaction arrived.

46
00:04:33,890 --> 00:04:39,157
We next preview a planned switch of the active cluster to Frankfurt. The dry

47
00:04:39,167 --> 00:04:45,250
run verifies the Cluster Set and target replica without changing roles. Both clusters must

48
00:04:45,260 --> 00:04:50,728
be reachable and transaction state must be suitable. This is a controlled switchover, not

49
00:04:50,738 --> 00:04:52,650
emergency forced failover.

50
00:04:54,680 --> 00:05:02,338
The replay now executes the planned switchover. MySQL Shell synchronizes the target, coordinates the

51
00:05:02,348 --> 00:05:09,883
role change, and updates the managed replication topology. Router behavior follows Cluster Set metadata

52
00:05:09,893 --> 00:05:15,534
and routing policy. The left and right terminals will now verify the resulting roles.

53
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:23,045
The final role checks are made against each direct database. Frankfurt is now the

54
00:05:23,055 --> 00:05:29,790
writable global primary. London is read only and receives the managed asynchronous stream. ONLINE

55
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:34,994
status must be retained at both sites, and global Cluster Set health must be

56
00:05:35,004 --> 00:05:35,506
healthy.

57
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:42,700
A new transaction on Frankfurt is now read directly on London. This proves the

58
00:05:42,710 --> 00:05:49,211
new replication direction in the instructional sequence. Keep the network path, service discovery, local

59
00:05:49,221 --> 00:05:54,369
reconciliation, and database replication checks separate when operating the real deployment.

60
00:05:56,520 --> 00:06:01,237
The console views in this video were captured read only from the existing deployment

61
00:06:01,247 --> 00:06:08,520
and redacted. Creation, SQL writes, and the Frankfurt switchover were illustrative replay scenes, not

62
00:06:08,530 --> 00:06:16,664
live operations. Submariner carries the traffic. Lighthouse provides service discovery. Each Operator reconciles locally.

63
00:06:16,674 --> 00:06:21,216
MySQL Cluster Set controls database replication and global roles.
